{"name":"Vanilla Flower","hex":"#e9dfcf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vanilla","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tropical climbing orchid of the genus Vanilla (especially Vanilla planifolia), bearing podlike fruit yielding an extract used in flavoring food or in perfumes.","The fruit or bean of the vanilla plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of vanilla.","Standard, plain, default, unmodified, basic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vanilla"},{"term":"flower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colorful, conspicuous structure associated with angiosperms, frequently scented and attracting various insects, and which may or may not be used for sexual reproduction.","A reproductive structure in angiosperms (flowering plants), often conspicuously colourful and typically including sepals, petals, and either or both stamens and/or a pistil."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put forth blooms.","To decorate with pictures of flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vanilla","blocks":[{"text":"Vanilla is a spice derived from orchids of the genus Vanilla, primarily obtained from the seed pods of the flat-leaved Mexican and Central American vanilla (V. planifolia)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Vanilla_planifolia_%286998639597%29.jpg/330px-Vanilla_planifolia_%286998639597%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower"},{"title":"Vanilla (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color vanilla is a rich tint of off-white as well as a medium pale tint of yellow."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Vanilla_planifolia_1.jpg/330px-Vanilla_planifolia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Vanilla_6beans.JPG/500px-Vanilla_6beans.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Vanilla_6beans.JPG/500px-Vanilla_6beans.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dried vanilla beans","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanilla_6beans.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Vanilla_florentine_codex.jpg/500px-Vanilla_florentine_codex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Vanilla_florentine_codex.jpg/500px-Vanilla_florentine_codex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Drawing of the Vanilla plant from the Florentine Codex (c. 1580) and description of its use and properties written in the Nahuatl language","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanilla_florentine_codex.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Vanilla_fragrans_3.jpg/500px-Vanilla_fragrans_3.jpg?utm_